A||||16||deceler8||My take on ultimate dog bone...||||10
Z||000000||deceler8||10-25-1999||03:47 PM||||Last week, while I was making a new shift console out of oak to replace my crappy original (easy, and a hell of a lot cheaper than the $75 quoted by Kick Hill), I was looking at some of the scraps from my project and wondered, "Why not wood dogbone bushings"?<P>I used some oak scraps and an adjustable hole cutter, cut some stock of the appropriate thickness, and tapped them for the steel sleeves from the original rubber bushings and voila, the oak ultimate dogbone.<P>I did start with a bone out of the junkyard rather than the one on my car. I cleaned it and painted it with engine enamel prior to putting the bushings in.<P>Total cost of project, including junkyard bone...about $4.<P>They are significantly stiffer and have eliminated a lot of the noise from the engine compartment caused by the engine moving around, but the vibration increased significantly at idle.<P>After the first drive with the new dogbone, I now know where most of my noises are coming from (seems like inside the doors). Next project is to fix these noises.<P>Am I nuts ? Is there a possible downside to this project ? Don't throw flammability at me...oak probably has a higher ignition point than urethane or the rubber originals.||||reg||10
Z||000001||Terrybogin||10-25-1999||06:54 PM||||Now we can honestly say that deceler8's Fiero is sporting a Woody.||||reg||10
Z||000002||RICK||10-25-1999||07:07 PM||||I would think that after a while with the engine putting stress on the wood (rocking back and fourth) that the wood would start to crack.  And thats bad to have a cracked woody (so I've heard).||||reg||1
Z||000003||hefty||10-25-1999||09:37 PM||||wow, oak, now that is custom. I wouldn't think that oak would crack under the conditions, I do think it would "wallow" out or wear out faster than a synthetic.||||reg||1
Z||000004||lowCG||10-26-1999||02:05 AM||||Which way did you align the grain?<BR>Like a baseball bat,you never want to hit the ball off the stamp.<BR>Morgans had(probably still have)wood frames,probably ash or something.<BR>What's next?<BR>Maybe a shiftknob,or how about a full on woody with surfboards on the roof?||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||10-26-1999||09:22 AM||||The only concern I'd have would be cracking the bracket on the block. you didn't say what motor/year it is and some setups have weaker brakets than others. the timber is so ridged that there is nothing to take vibration.<P>And yes you can crack off a braket. It's not as common on Fiero as it is on some FWD cars because the strut is be pushed on instead of pulled.<P>How long will it last? heck if I know. I'll ask the termites.||||reg||1
Z||000006||deceler8||10-26-1999||10:00 AM||||It's an '86 2m4, 2.5 liter.<P>I did make cross-grain cuts in the oak to minimize the possibility of cracking, but I may just change out one of the bushings back to rubber to cut down on the vibration or look for rubber sleeves to replace the steel ones.<P>Does the poly bushing give very much or absorb much vibration ? The ones I've seen have been about as hard as rocks and I can't imagine they're much softer than wood.<P>Still thinking about other oak accessories for the Fiero...a shift knob would be nice, but mine's an automatic and that might be a little tricky. Might experiment with lower dash corners...||||reg||11
Z||000007||theogre||10-26-1999||01:46 PM||||the poly bushings are hard but still some give to them.<P>Is yours the automatic tranny? does it have only one tranny mount under a huge steel bracket. They started that in 86.5 as far as I can tell.... really crappy mount... You'll always get more vibration in these than the ones with 2 tranny mounts.<P>I don't think the serpent belt started untill 87, so yours should have the big bracket that holds the alternator and the strut end. those are toughter than the newer ones.<P>I have the 87 2.5 auto setup, and the strut is the only thing keeping the motor from flopping over. The OEM strut blocks vibration, but it's so soft I have 2+ inches of travel in the motor. It's broke the exhaust twice from the pounding. (I tried both the new and old style struts. same results)<P>With the new poly bushings I get more vibration, probly not as much as the wood ones, but now the engine isn't beatting itself apart. Less than one inch of travel now as far as I can tell. (Travel was measrued between the trunk edge, and the PCV valve with the car loading in both directions.)||||reg||1
Z||000008||lowCG||10-26-1999||01:50 PM||||The poly would be a step up in the comfort dept.from wood,not by much though.Your idea of rubber on one end is a good one,had a new,dealer dogbone on my 4cyl,and it kept up fine,had new mounts throughout also.<BR>Holes can be drilled in the poly to give a little movement/comfort,but you can't go back once they're there.<BR>Now on my 6cyl,I use some very firm synthetic rubber from some bumpstops,works great,but the rest of the mounts are squishy from an old oil leak.||||reg||1
Z||000009||deceler8||10-26-1999||02:37 PM||||Hey Ogre...just a question...Should the bolts on the front of the bone be tight enough to prevent movement or should they be loose to allow the strut to travel in the slot ?<P>There's a ton of movement with the old bone (rubber) if the bolts are loose in the front to allow travel, but probably not more than an inch or so even with the stock bone and these bolts thoroughly tightened.<P>I assumed the slot is to allow for equilibrium in the mounts prior to tightening them. With the bolts loose, the front bolt settles right in the center of the slot with no load on the car. So that's where I tightened them.<P>Also to kill vibration on the oakbone, I removed the steel sleeves and replaced them with a section of 1/2" heater hose...vibration gone and still minimal movement.<P>Is necessity the mother of invention, or what?||||reg||5
Z||000010||mrfixit58||10-26-1999||04:20 PM||||I like your simplistic approach to the soultion. So what if it fractures in a few thousand miles. It's cheap and you cneasily make a new one. Might be a good idea to check at oil change intervals.<P>Just one additinal note reguarding the combustion point of oak.... did you know that the cable cars in San Francisco use oak slabs for their brakes? Take one look at the downhill runs and your solution seems like a safe bet.||||reg||14
Z||000011||Formula||10-26-1999||04:49 PM||||oak brakes? Explain this to me.||||reg||1
Z||000012||theogre||10-26-1999||05:08 PM||||the slot is only for adjustment.<P><BR>on most fiero you want the trany in neutral and the car on flat ground. the strut should wind up someplace in the slot. If it doesn't you want to check condition/adjustment of the motor and tranny mounts. (Motor/tranny mounts usually have at least one direction of movment in the mounting holes.)on the setups with 3-4 motor & tranny mounts you want to avoid preloading the mounts if you can. <P><BR>the problem child is the 86.5+ cars with 4 cyl and automatic tranny. These have only one tranny mount. These don't have a "home" position. The strut is all that holds the thing upright. Never Ever run one of these setups with a bad strut. You will break things. Usually expencive things. The only way to adjust these is guess. I usually push mine so the motor is as far forward as it goes. This keeps the air cleaner as far from the trunk wall as posible. even then it can hit with the OEM struts. Having the air cleaner on the 4 cyl hit the trunk wall can tear the TBI unit off the manifold.||||reg||1
Z||000013||lowCG||10-26-1999||05:54 PM||||So,how do your bolts function in a slot without the sleeve to tighten against?<BR>Maybe you need to bore out the oak,and use a larger sized piece of hose over the sleeve,to keep it all in place.||||reg||1
Z||000014||deceler8||10-26-1999||06:20 PM||||The oak bushings are the same width as the original steel sleeve...I used a fender washer (thin) between the oak and the bracket on each side.<P>I used oak, but I wonder if a softer wood, made in the same manner, would work and at the same time reduce some of the vibration.<P>Maybe I'll try some Southern yellow pine next time. Or just buy the urethane.||||reg||3
Z||000015||Phil||10-26-1999||08:50 PM||||Howsabout rubber tree wood?||||reg||1
Z||000016||lowCG||10-26-1999||10:17 PM||||Steal some kid's skateboard,and make do with the wheels.<P>Saw one at the Goodwill for a couple bucks today too.Skateboard wheels make good bumpstops too.<BR>The wood,like many plastics,will tend to take a set with time and become sloppy(not enough memory).||||reg||11
